---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import SourcesStandards from '../../components/SourcesStandards.astro';
import CalculatorDisclaimer from '../../components/CalculatorDisclaimer.astro';
import { calculatorWebPage } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Where does the 35.5 ppg constant come from?',
    a: 'It represents barite\'s own absolute density expressed in ppg (a specific gravity of roughly 4.2-4.3, the standard API barite weighting material) — the theoretical mud weight if the mud were pure barite with no water or oil phase at all. The formula works because it\'s finding what fraction of that theoretical maximum needs adding to reach the target weight.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this account for volume increase from adding barite?',
    a: 'No — this gives the weight of barite (in lb per barrel of starting mud) needed to reach the target weight, treating the added solids as if they replace an equivalent liquid volume. A full mud engineering calculation also tracks the resulting volume increase and solids content; this calculator answers the weighting-material question specifically, not total system volume.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many sacks of barite does the lb/bbl figure translate into?',
    a: 'Divide the total pounds needed (lb/bbl × system volume in bbl) by the sack weight your supplier actually uses — commonly 100 lb per sack in the US, though some regions still use other sizes. Always confirm the sack weight on the delivery ticket rather than assuming, since ordering against the wrong sack size under- or over-orders material by a real margin.',
  },
];

const webAppLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/barite-required#app",
  "name": "Barite Required Calculator: Weighting Up Drilling Mud",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/barite-required",
  "description": "Free calculator for how much barite, in pounds per barrel, is needed to raise drilling mud from one weight to a heavier target weight.",
  "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
  "applicationSubCategory": "Drilling engineering calculator",
  "operatingSystem": "Any (web browser)",
  "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isAccessibleForFree": true,
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#organization" }
};

const webPageLd = calculatorWebPage(webAppLd, { lang: 'en' });

---
<BaseLayout
  title="Barite Required Calculator: Weighting Up Drilling Mud"
  description="Free calculator for how much barite, in pounds per barrel, is needed to raise drilling mud from one weight to a heavier target weight."
  extraJsonLd={[webAppLd, webPageLd]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' }, { label: 'Barite Required', href: '/calculators/barite-required' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/calculators/barite-required"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/calculators/barite-required" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Calculator</div>
    <h1>Barite Required <span>to Raise Mud Weight</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">How many pounds of barite per barrel of starting mud are needed to weight up from a current mud weight to a heavier target weight.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="calc-box">
          <div class="calc-title">🧭 Weight-Up Inputs</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="br-mw1">Current Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
              <input type="number" id="br-mw1" value="11" step="0.1">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="br-mw2">Target Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
              <input type="number" id="br-mw2" value="12" step="0.1">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-output visible" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:16px;">
            <div class="output-line headline" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">Barite Required</span><span class="output-value" id="out-barite">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <CalculatorDisclaimer lang="en" context="Confirm the mud system's actual volume and the barite's own measured specific gravity before ordering material for a real weight-up — field barite assay varies around the nominal 35.5 ppg API figure, and treating a lighter actual batch as if it were nominal under-orders material." />
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The Formula Used</h2>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Barite Required</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Barite (lb/bbl) = 1500 × (Target MW − Current MW) ÷ (35.5 − Target MW)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Both mud weights in ppg. Result is pounds of barite per barrel of starting mud.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>Worked Example</h3>
        <p>Weighting up a 500 bbl active system from 10.5 ppg to a 12.0 ppg kill mud weight:</p>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Barite Required</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Barite = 1500 × (12.0 − 10.5) ÷ (35.5 − 12.0) = 1500 × 1.5 ÷ 23.5 = 95.7 lb/bbl</code></dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block mt-24">
          <dt class="formula-label">Total for a 500 bbl System</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>95.7 lb/bbl × 500 bbl = 47,850 lb ≈ 479 sacks at 100 lb/sack</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Round up for mixing losses and to avoid a second trip to the pump room mid-weight-up.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>Where This Fits</h3>
        <p>This is the practical weighting-up step behind several other calculators on the site: the mud engineer needs this figure any time a <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet">kill sheet</a> or the <a href="/calculators/mamw">MAMW calculator</a> calls for a heavier kill mud weight than what's currently in the pits, or any time the <a href="/calculators/maasp">MAASP</a> or mud weight window calls for weighting up ahead of drilling into a higher-pressure zone. As the target weight gets closer to barite's own 35.5 ppg absolute density, the denominator shrinks and the required quantity per barrel climbs sharply — a real practical ceiling on how heavy a water- or oil-based mud can realistically be weighted with barite alone. Mixing additives and hitting a target mud weight is routine mud-system work — for pay and the complete job breakdown, see the <a href="/careers/derrickhand">derrickhand career guide</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SourcesStandards lang="en" ids={['api13c']} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .output-line.headline .output-value { color: var(--amber); font-size: 18px; }
</style>

<script is:inline src="/scripts/calculators/barite-required.js" defer></script>
